  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 122 PM MDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nRelatively quiet conditions expected for the rest of the day today, \naside from an isolated threat of storm for the far northeastern \nplains this late afternoon. With the upper level ridge \nrestrengthening over the desert southwest, heat has begun to build \nover the plains today, and will likely continue to build through the \nweekend. Despite increased smoke concentration, we have still been \nable to warm up to the 90s by noon, and will likely reach mid 90s by \nthe end of the day. \n\nIn terms of any storm threat this afternoon/evening, surface \nobservations shows an axis of low-level moisture reaching up to the \nfar northeastern plains, with dewpoint temperatures in the low 60s. \nModerate instability will be in place, as evident in modeled Skew-T \nsoundings that show MLCAPE of 1400-1800 J/kg. While most CAMs keep \nour forecast area capped and dry, some models do indicate an \nisolated storm initiating in Nebraska and trekking down into \nSedgwick and Phillips counties. Confidence is low on this \noccurring, especially given the lack of any forcing in the area. \nHowever, have upped PoPs (10-20% chance) to account for that \npossibility. If a storm is able to develop, strong out flow winds \nof 40-50 mph and hail will be the main concerns. \n\nFriday will be similar to today, with temperatures reaching up to \nthe mid-90s again and smoke creating hazy skies. There will be \nelevated to locally critical fire weather conditions for the \nmountains and high valleys given how dry we will be (relative \nhumidity values between 8-15%), however winds should remain below \ncritical thresholds for the most part. \n\nThe upper level ridge will continue to strengthen through the \nweekend, with the axis of the mid-level thermal ridge shifting into \neastern Colorado by Saturday/Sunday. 700-mb temperatures are \nexpected to reach up to 19-22 C, resulting in highs soaring into \nthe 100s for most of the plains. Given HeatRisk levels reaching up\nto 3 (correlating to high risk for much of the population, \nespecially those who are heat sensitive and those without \neffective cooling/hydration), have opted in to issue a Heat \nAdvisory for the Denver metro and portions of the Urban corridor, \nincluding Boulder. Overnight lows should dip into the 60s Saturday\nnight so decided against on the Heat Advisory going into the\novernight hours of Saturday. However, with temperatures soaring \nto the high 90s/low 100s again on Sunday, another Heat Advisory \nwill likely be needed.\n\nAnother concern for Saturday will be elevated to locally critical \nfire weather conditions for the northern mountains and high valleys. \nRelative humidity values should drop to 9-15% by the afternoon, with \nwest/ northwesterly winds gusting up to 20-25 mph. There is still \nsome uncertainty in magnitude and duration of the stronger wind gusts,\nso have refrained from any highlights. However, if we continue to\ntrend towards gusts of 20-30 mph for a large portion of the day, \nthen a Red Flag Warning will be needed. \n\nWith moisture slightly increasing by Sunday (still below normal \nprecipitable water values), afternoon showers and thunderstorm \nchances begin to increase for the mountains. Towards the beginning \nof next week, guidance does have the upper level high weakening, but \nmodels continue to differ on exact evolution of the ridge. Will it \nreside in the Four Corners region or track more east closer to \nTexas? It is still uncertain right now, but this placement will \naffect how much monsoonal moisture we receive towards midweek. \nThe ECMWF favors a weaker ridge with the axis shifting more east, \nand thus better moisture for us. The GFS, however, favors a more \nwesterly placement of the high, and thus less moisture transport \ninto the region. It is interesting to note that the AIGFS aligns \nmore with the ECMWF, so will be keeping watch to see if future \nruns of the GFS changes. Regardless, we are expecting increasing \nchances for precipitation for the mountains and plains towards the\nend of next week.",
